# Releasing {{PROJECT_NAME}}

<!-- Adapt this guide to the actual release process before copying it to
docs/RELEASING.md. Replace every placeholder and remove editing instructions.
Adopting this guide does not create a release or a tag. -->

## Responsibilities and versioning

Release maintainers: {{RELEASE_MAINTAINERS}}

Version policy: {{VERSIONING_POLICY}}

Tag format: {{RELEASE_TAG_FORMAT}}

Distribution channels and artifacts: {{DISTRIBUTION_CHANNELS_AND_ARTIFACTS}}

Document which interfaces are covered by compatibility promises, how breaking
changes are communicated, and how prereleases are identified. Use the project's
actual version policy; do not claim Semantic Versioning unless it is followed.

## Prepare the release

1. Identify the reviewed commit to release and confirm the required CI checks
   passed. Check unresolved release blockers and relevant security reports.
2. Update versions in the project's actual source and package metadata, and
   update lockfiles only as required by its tooling. Record migration instructions
   for breaking changes and update installation examples when needed.
3. Review [CHANGELOG.md](../CHANGELOG.md) and prepare a release section with the
   intended version. Use the actual publication date when publishing; keep work
   that is not included under Unreleased. Review generated release notes for
   accuracy, attribution, and any private security information.
4. Build and test the artifacts that users will receive, including supported
   platforms. Confirm the archives/packages contain LICENSE and all required
   third-party licenses and notices. Generate checksums, an SBOM, signatures,
   or provenance if the project's distribution process requires them.

Project-specific verification commands:

```text
{{RELEASE_VERIFICATION_COMMANDS}}
```

## Publish

When a release is requested, follow the project's publishing procedure using
the reviewed commit and authorized publishing identity:

```text
{{PUBLISHING_PROCEDURE}}
```

Create the intended release tag as part of that procedure, publish the matching
artifacts and notes, and verify that the registry/download links point to the
expected version. Keep credentials out of logs, commits, and release artifacts.

## Verify and follow up

- Install or download the published version into a clean environment and run the
  documented quick start. Check the reported version and supported upgrade path.
- Confirm release notes, checksums, required notices, and links are accessible.
- Prepare the next Unreleased section and any development-version changes.
- If a published artifact is faulty, document the issue and use the registry's
  supported correction, withdrawal, or new-version process. Avoid silently
  replacing published artifacts or moving an existing release tag.
